/// Chapter 7 of the guide, written from md-docs/learn/07-state-and-reactivity.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page Learn07StateAndReactivity(path: "/learn/07-state-and-reactivity", title: "State and reactivity", description: "state, derived, effect, actions and let, persist, timers, automatic clean-up, element handles and the browser's values.", layout: DocsShell("State and reactivity", kind: .tutorial, chapter: t("nav.group.language"), blurb: "A state is a signal, and only what reads it updates: no tree re-renders, no subscriptions to manage.", source: "md-docs/learn/07-state-and-reactivity.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "state", label: "state"), Anchor(id: "derived", label: "derived"), Anchor(id: "effect", label: "effect"), Anchor(id: "actions-and-locals", label: "Actions and locals"), Anchor(id: "persist", label: "persist"), Anchor(id: "timers", label: "Timers"), Anchor(id: "automatic-clean-up", label: "Automatic clean-up"), Anchor(id: "element-handles", label: "Element handles"), Anchor(id: "the-browser-as-values", label: "The browser as values"), Anchor(id: "where-values-come-from-in-order", label: "Where values come from, in order"), Anchor(id: "summary", label: "Summary"), Anchor(id: "quiz", label: "Quiz"), Anchor(id: "common-mistakes", label: "Common mistakes")], learned: ["Declare a `state` and change it from a handler or an action", "Work a value out with `derived` instead of keeping a second state in step", "Reach outside the page with an `effect` and its `cleanup`", "Keep a value across visits with `persist`, and run timers with `every` and `after`", "Read `viewport`, `query`, `now` and the browser's other values as live values"], prevTo: "/learn/06-elements", prevLabel: t("ch.06"), nextTo: "/learn/08-events", nextLabel: t("ch.08"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter covers state and reactivity in WebFluent. A `state` is a value the page can change. Anything that reads it — a text, an attribute, a `derived` value, an `effect`, a style splice — updates when it changes, and nothing else does. This is *fine-grained reactivity*: there is no re-render of a component tree, and you never subscribe or unsubscribe by hand.\n\nIn this chapter:\n\n- `state`, `derived` and `effect` — the three kinds of reactive value\n- actions and `let` locals — the code that changes state\n- `persist`, timers and element handles\n- how the runtime cleans up after a page, a branch or a list item\n- the browser's own values: `viewport`, `query`, `now` and the rest\n- where a name is looked up, and the mistakes people make")

        Heading("state", id: "state").h2

        Markdown("You declare a state at the top of a page, component or store body, with an initial value. This counter shows the whole idea — one state, a text that reads it, and a button that writes it:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Counter(path: "/", title: "Counter", description: "A state, read and written.") {
    state count = 0                  // inferred: Number
    state name: String = "Sam"       // declared
    state items: [String] = []       // a list of strings
    state selected: String? = null   // may be null
    state user = { name: "Sam", tags: ["admin"] }   // a map literal: its fields are its type

    Heading("Counter").h1
    Text("{name} has clicked {count} times")
    Button("+1") { on click { count = count + 1 } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Each click sets `count`, and only the `Text` that reads it changes.")

        Callout("**Coming from React?** `useState` returns a value and a setter, and a change runs the whole component again. A `state` is assigned directly, `count = count + 1`, and the body never runs again: only what reads `count` updates. A value that should follow a prop or a state is a `derived`. See [Coming from React](/coming-from/react#things-that-work-differently).", kind: .note)

        Markdown("The type of a state is read off its initial value, or written after a colon. [Types](/learn/12-types) has the type language. Assigning a value of another type is an error (`T01`).\n\nYou write state only in an imperative block — a handler, an action or an effect. You can:\n\n- assign it: `count = count + 1`\n- change a place inside it: `user.name = \"Ann\"`, `items[0] = \"Pen\"`\n- call a method that changes it: `items.push(\"Pen\")`, `items.sort()`\n\nA change inside a value gives the state a new copy with that place changed, so everything that reads it repaints, and a `persist` value is written.\n\nThe *static paint* — the HTML the build pre-renders for a page — seeds every state with its initial value, so a pre-rendered page shows what the first paint in the browser would.")

        Heading("derived", id: "derived").h2

        Markdown("A derived value is computed from state, and computed again when any state it read changes. This cart works out its subtotal, total and whether it is empty:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Cart(path: "/", title: "Cart", description: "Totals worked out from state.") {
    state items = [{ name: "Pen", price: 2.5, qty: 4 }, { name: "Pad", price: 6, qty: 1 }]
    state taxRate = 0.2
    derived subtotal = items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price * i.qty, 0)
    derived total = subtotal * (1 + taxRate)
    derived empty = items.length == 0

    Heading("Your cart").h1
    Text("Subtotal: {format(subtotal, .currency)}")
    Text("Total: {format(total, .currency)}")
    if empty { Text("Your cart is empty.").muted }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The rules:\n\n- A derived value may read state, other derived values and the browser's values, and may call an action that returns a value.\n- It is never assigned to (`X01`), and an action it calls may not assign state (`X04`).\n- The static paint seeds it from the initial state.")

        Heading("effect", id: "effect").h2

        Markdown("An effect runs once, then again whenever a state it read changes. Its `cleanup \{ \}` block runs before the next run, and when the page — or the branch the effect lives in — leaves:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Watch(path: "/", title: "Watch", description: "Effects that follow a search.") {
    state search = ""
    effect {
        log("search is now {search}")
    }
    effect {
        document.title = "Search: {search}"
        cleanup { document.title = "Search" }
    }
    Heading("Watch").h1
    Input(bind: search, placeholder: "Search")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Use an effect for the world outside the page: the document title, a third-party widget, a subscription. Keep computation in `derived`.")

        Callout("An effect that writes what it reads — `effect \{ n = n + 1 \}` — starts its own next run and never settles. The compiler refuses it (`X02`). Compute the value with `derived` instead.", kind: .warning)

        Heading("Actions and locals", id: "actions-and-locals").h2

        Markdown("An action is a named function of a page, component or store. Its body is an imperative block: assignments, `let` locals, calls, `if`, `for`, `try`, `return` and `await`. This to-do list adds, removes and loads items with three actions:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Todos(path: "/", title: "To-dos", description: "Actions that change a list.") {
    state items: [String] = []
    state draft = ""

    action add() {
        let text = draft.trim()
        if text == "" { return }
        items.push(text)
        draft = ""
    }

    action remove(index: Number) {
        items = items.filter((_, i) => i != index)
    }

    action load() {
        let r = await fetch("/api/todos")
        let { todos } = r
        items = todos
    }

    Heading("To-dos").h1
    Input(bind: draft, placeholder: "What needs doing?") { on keydown(e) { if e.key == "Enter" { add() } } }
    Button("Add").primary { on click { add() } }
    for item, i in items {
        Row { Text(item)  IconButton(icon: "trash", label: "Remove") { on click { remove(i) } } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("What an action's body can hold:\n\n- **Locals.** `let x = …` declares a local of the action or handler. `let \{ a, b \} = m` and `let [x, y] = l` destructure a map and a list.\n- **`await`.** Using it makes the action asynchronous, and `name.pending` is `true` while a call of it runs ([Forms](/learn/14-forms#show-that-a-request-is-running)).\n- **Loops.** `for item in list \{ \}`, `for item, i in list \{ \}` and `for n in 1..=10 \{ \}`.\n- **Errors.** `try \{ \} catch e \{ \}` guards a call that may fail.\n- **`return`.** `return` leaves the action; `return x` leaves with a value.\n\nA `let` is the action's own. After `let list = items`, `list.push(x)` changes `list` and leaves `items` as it was; assign it back with `items = list`, or write `items.push(x)`, which gives the state a new list.\n\nYou call a page's action by name, `add()`, and a store's through the store, `Todos.add()`.")

        Heading("persist", id: "persist").h2

        Markdown("`persist` declares a state kept in the browser's storage across visits. It is read back on the next visit and written on every change. These two preferences survive a reload:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Prefs(path: "/", title: "Preferences", description: "Settings kept across visits.") {
    persist scheme = "light"
    persist perPage = 20
    Heading("Preferences").h1
    Select(bind: scheme, label: "Theme") {
        Select.Option("Light", value: "light")
        Select.Option("Dark", value: "dark")
    }
    Slider(bind: perPage, min: 10, max: 100, step: 10, label: "Rows per page")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("How it behaves:\n\n- The storage key is the owner's name and the state's (`Prefs.scheme`), so a page and a store may each have a value of the same name.\n- It works in a store too, where every page shares it.\n- When storage is blocked or full, the value falls back to its initial value.\n- A block after the declaration can say which storage to use, give a version with migrations, and choose whether to follow other tabs. That block and the rest of the policy are in [Stores](/learn/11-stores#keeping-a-value-across-visits).")

        Heading("Timers", id: "timers").h2

        Markdown("`every(ms) \{ \}` repeats, and `after(ms) \{ \}` fires once. The interval is a number of milliseconds or a duration: `every(5.seconds)`, `after(2.minutes)`. This page counts the seconds it has been open and hides a welcome message after three:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Clock(path: "/", title: "Clock", description: "A timer and a one-off delay.") {
    state seconds = 0
    state toast: String? = "Welcome"
    every(1000) { seconds = seconds + 1 }
    after(3000) { toast = null }
    Heading("Clock").h1
    Text("{seconds}s on this page")
    if let message = toast { Alert(message).info }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Both stop when what declared them leaves the page: a branch that closes, a list item that is removed, a route that changes.")

        Heading("Automatic clean-up", id: "automatic-clean-up").h2

        Markdown("Everything a body creates — effects, timers, listeners, element handles — belongs to the *scope* it was created in: the page, branch, list item, `match` arm or slot whose nodes it sits among. When that scope's nodes leave, the runtime disposes of what it created:\n\n- an `if` branch turns false, an item drops out of a `for`, a `match` arm changes, or the route changes;\n- intervals are cleared, effects stop running, and `cleanup \{ \}` blocks run.\n\nYou never unsubscribe by hand.")

        Heading("Element handles", id: "element-handles").h2

        Markdown("`ref: name` on an element gives you a *handle*: a name that reads as the element itself. Here a button and the `/` key both put the cursor in the search box:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Search(path: "/", title: "Search", description: "A handle on an input.") {
    state q = ""
    Heading("Search").h1
    Input(bind: q, ref: box, placeholder: "Search")
    Button("Focus the box") { on click { box.focus() } }
    Button("Clear") { on click { q = ""  box.focus() } }
    on key("/") { box.focus() }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The `ref:` declares the name; you do not declare it anywhere else. Anything the element has is there: `box.value`, `box.scrollIntoView()`.\n\nA handle is filled once the element exists, so set-up code at the top of the body cannot use it yet. Read it in a handler, an `effect` or `after(0) \{ \}`.")

        Heading("The browser as values", id: "the-browser-as-values").h2

        Markdown("Some names are always in scope and kept current by the runtime, so a page reads the browser the way it reads its own state:")

        Table(caption: "The browser as values") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Name").header
                    Table.Cell("Is").header
                    Table.Cell("Updates when").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`viewport`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.width`, `.height`, and `.sm` `.md` `.lg` `.xl` — whether the window is at least that breakpoint") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("a breakpoint is crossed (through `matchMedia`, not on every pixel)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`query`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the URL's query string as a map: `query.tab` for `?tab=x`") }
                    Table.Cell("the URL changes")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`hash`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the URL's `#fragment`, without the `#`") }
                    Table.Cell("the URL changes")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`theme`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"light\"`, `\"dark\"` or `\"system\"` — the reader's choice ([Styling](/learn/13-styling#dark-mode))") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`setTheme(…)` is called") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`now`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("a `DateTime`, every minute; `now(every: 1.seconds)` for a finer clock ([Types](/learn/12-types#now))") }
                    Table.Cell("the clock ticks")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`network`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.online`, `.effectiveType`, `.saveData`, `.downlink`, `.queued` ([Real-time](/recipes/show-the-network))") }
                    Table.Cell("the connection changes")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`update`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.available` and `.apply()` — a new version of an offline site ([Offer a new version](/recipes/offer-a-new-version))") }
                    Table.Cell("a new build is installed")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`locale`, `dir`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the current locale and `\"ltr\"`/`\"rtl\"`, with i18n configured ([Translate a site](/recipes/translate-a-site))") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`setLocale(…)` is called") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("This page reads four of them:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Where(path: "/", title: "Where", description: "The browser, read as values.") {
    Heading("Where am I?").h1
    Text(if viewport.md { "A wide window" } else { "A narrow window" })
    derived tab = query.tab ?? "all"
    Text("Tab: {tab}, section: {hash}")
    Text("Theme: {theme}")
    if !network.online { Alert("You are offline.").warning }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The rules:\n\n- The breakpoints are the `screen-sm` … `screen-xl` design tokens: 640, 768, 1024 and 1280px unless a theme moves them.\n- They are the language's names, so a `state`, prop or `const` of the same name is an error (`E118`).\n- Copy one into a `state` and you keep the value it had when the page opened: `state clock = now(every: 1.seconds)` never ticks (`X06`). Read it where it is shown, or in a `derived`.\n- The static paint does not know them, so what depends on them is drawn once the page is live. For layout, prefer [responsive values](/learn/13-styling#responsive-values), which are right in the first paint.")

        Heading("Where values come from, in order", id: "where-values-come-from-in-order").h2

        Markdown("When a body reads a name, the nearest declaration wins. The compiler looks in this order, innermost first:\n\n1. A `let` local, a lambda's parameter or a caught error, inside its block.\n2. Loop variables and arm bindings (`if let`, a `match` arm), inside their block.\n3. Route parameters and props (`slug`, `_ label`).\n4. `state`, `persist`, `derived`, `resource` and actions of the body. A body member may not share a name with a parameter or a prop (`E102`).\n5. A `use`d store's members, always through the store's name (`Cart.total`).\n6. Constants, `data` and `image` declarations, `env.X`, and the browser's values above.\n7. Browser globals: `window`, `document`, `localStorage`, `JSON`, `Math`, `Date`, `fetch`, `setTimeout`, `console`, `navigator`, `location`.\n\nSo with `state name = \"outer\"`, `for name in items by name \{ Text(name) \}` shows each item, and a `const name` is hidden by both.")

        Heading("Summary", id: "summary").h2

        Table(caption: "Summary") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("You write").header
                    Table.Cell("It is").header
                    Table.Cell("Section").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`state count = 0`") }
                    Table.Cell("a value the page changes; its readers update")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[`state`](#state)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`derived total = …`") }
                    Table.Cell("a value computed from state, never assigned")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[`derived`](#derived)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`effect \{ … cleanup \{ … \} \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("code that runs when what it read changes")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[`effect`](#effect)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`action add() \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("a named function that changes state")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Actions and locals](#actions-and-locals)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`let x = …`") }
                    Table.Cell("a local of an action or handler")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Actions and locals](#actions-and-locals)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`persist scheme = \"light\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("a state kept across visits")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[`persist`](#persist)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`every(1000) \{ … \}`, `after(3000) \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("a repeating timer and a one-off delay")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Timers](#timers)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`ref: box`") }
                    Table.Cell("a handle on an element")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Element handles](#element-handles)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`viewport`, `query`, `hash`, `theme`, `now`, `network`, `update`, `locale`, `dir`") }
                    Table.Cell("the browser, read as values")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[The browser as values](#the-browser-as-values)") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("[Grammar](/reference/grammar) and [Built-in functions](/reference/built-ins) are the reference for each.")

        Heading("Quiz", id: "quiz").h2

        Quiz("A page shows `Heading(\"Counter\")` and `Text(\"\{count\}\")`. A click runs `count = count + 1`. What runs again?", id: "State and reactivity#12", choices: [
            QuizChoice(text: "The page's body, which draws the heading and the text again", right: false, why: "A body runs once, when the page opens; nothing re-renders after it."),
            QuizChoice(text: "The `Text` that reads `count`, and nothing else", right: true, why: "Only what reads a state follows it; the heading reads no state."),
            QuizChoice(text: "The page and every component inside it", right: false, why: "There is no component tree to re-render; a component's body runs once too.")
        ])

        Quiz("`items` is a state. Which `total` stays equal to the sum of `items` as items are added?", id: "State and reactivity#12", choices: [
            QuizChoice(text: "`state total = items.sum()`", right: false, why: "A state takes its initial value once; adding an item leaves the total as it was."),
            QuizChoice(text: "`derived total = items.sum()`", right: true, why: "A derived value is worked out again whenever a state it read changes."),
            QuizChoice(text: "`derived total = 0`, with `total = items.sum()` in each action that adds an item", right: false, why: "A derived value is never assigned (X01).")
        ])

        Quiz("Which of these clocks ticks every second?", id: "State and reactivity#12", choices: [
            QuizChoice(text: "`state clock = now(every: 1.seconds)`", right: false, why: "A state takes `now` once, when the page opens (X06)."),
            QuizChoice(text: "`derived clock = now(every: 1.seconds)`", right: true, why: "A derived value follows `now` as it ticks."),
            QuizChoice(text: "`derived clock = now`", right: false, why: "This one ticks, but once a minute: `now` alone keeps minutes, and `now(every: 1.seconds)` asks for seconds.")
        ])

        Heading("Common mistakes", id: "common-mistakes").h2

        Markdown("**A state that copies `now` once.** A `state` takes its initial value when the page opens and keeps it, so a clock or an \"… ago\" worked out from it never moves:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Feed(path: "/", title: "Feed", description: "When this was posted.") {
    state posted = @2026-03-14T09:30:00Z
    state clock = now(every: 1.seconds)
    Heading("Feed").h1
    Text("Posted {posted.until(clock).minutes()} minutes ago")
}"#, title: "wf · draws X06")

        Markdown("Declare it `derived clock = now(every: 1.seconds)`. The same holds for `viewport`, `query`, `hash`, `theme`, `network` and `update` ([X06](/errors/X06)).\n\n**An effect that writes what it reads.** Each run changes `visits`, which starts the next run, so the page never settles:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Visits(path: "/", title: "Visits", description: "A count that runs away.") {
    state visits = 0
    effect { visits = visits + 1 }
    Heading("Visits").h1
    Text("{visits} visits")
}"#, title: "wf · draws X02")

        Markdown("Change the state in a handler or an action, or work the value out with `derived` ([X02](/errors/X02)).\n\n**Assigning a derived value.** A `derived` is worked out from what it reads, so nothing may set it:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Cart(path: "/", title: "Cart", description: "A total you cannot set.") {
    state items = [2.5, 6]
    derived total = items.sum()
    Heading("Cart").h1
    Text("Total: {total}")
    Button("Clear") { on click { total = 0 } }
}"#, title: "wf · draws X01")

        Markdown("Change what it reads instead, `items = []`, and `total` follows ([X01](/errors/X01)).")
    }
}
